TASK:{"title":"AERO9500 Final Report (Part 2): Space Mission Design","overview":"Develop a preliminary space-mission design that benefits Australia and report the mission concept and its space-segment elements. Use mission-design software of your choice, supported by hand calculations if useful, and justify the proposed design."}
RUBRIC:[{"id":"C1","name":"Introduction: mission concept and rationale. Include the following required information: What is the spacecraft's primary mission? Why is the mission needed? How many spacecraft are used and why? Who are the users (operator and customers)?","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Addresses all requirements to a high standard. Detailed descriptions and discussions are included. References to literature as appropriate."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Addresses some requirements. Descriptions may feature lack of specific details and/or incomplete referencing."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Many or all requirements are not addressed. Poor descriptions or lack of appropriate referencing."}]},{"id":"C2","name":"Main body: mission design. Present a summary of the mission design using software and/or hand calculations as appropriate. Include relevant screenshots and software files in the main body of the report and/or appendix. Include the following information: Where will it launch from, and using which launch vehicle? Why did you choose this option? What type of orbit will you use? What are its characteristics and orbital elements? Will you need to perform any orbital maneuvers? Which ground control facilities will you use? How often will your spacecraft be able to communicate with ground control? How long will the mission last, and what are your plans for EOL?","weight":60,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Demonstrates high level understanding of the design procedure. Design clearly addresses mission and course concepts. Major requirements are all addressed and are well supported with quality evidence (e.g. software screenshots, software files, hand calculations, references from literature). Content is comprehensive, accurate, and persuasive."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Demonstrates adequate understanding of the design procedure. Design may be inconsistent with regards to purpose and clarity of thought or feature errors. Major requirements are addressed but may lack some detail or are not well supported with evidence. Content may not be comprehensive and/or persuasive."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Demonstrates minimal understanding of the design procedure. Major requirements are not addressed and/or lack evidence. Content is incomplete or not persuasive."}]},{"id":"C3","name":"Conclusion","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Detailed and appropriate conclusions reached from the evidence provided in the report."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Some conclusions are reached but they may be generic or lack specific detail."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"No actual or useful conclusions reached."}]},{"id":"C4","name":"Report presentation","weight":20,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"The document follows a clear and logical engineering report structure. The report is easy to read: well-written, with good spelling and grammar, and appropriate language style. All aspects of formatting are consistent throughout the document. Executive summary (or abstract) provides a concise overview of the report. Graphical and tabular presentation of data is appropriate, clear and consistent. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are cited properly."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Document is not at a professional level but does make use of headings and subheadings to indicate document structure. The report may be difficult to read: writing is just ok, broad idea comes across; spelling and grammar have some flaws, not quite appropriate language style. Although figures and tables are labelled, the formatting is unclear and/or inconsistent to the extent that the reader can lose track of the context when reading. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are mostly cited correctly."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"The document is poorly structured, is not coherent or shows a lack of understanding of the purpose of its sections. Much effort is required to read and understand the report: writing is poor, many mistakes with spelling and grammar, and possibly inappropriate language style (e.g. too informal). Presentation is poor to the extent that it impedes reading of the document. Examples include inconsistent formatting, and unlabelled figures or tables. References are either not cited or cited inconsistently."}]}]
MARKING:{"requirements":[],"technical_issues":[{"where":"Executive Summary vs Launch","issue":"Executive summary claims a direct launch, but the body describes a piggyback rideshare on an unselected mission."},{"where":"Launch","issue":"The two-launch-windows-per-day argument based on Kourou latitude versus inclination is muddled and not quantified."},{"where":"Launch","issue":"Relies on a February 2021 spaceflight manifest and an unbuilt Vega E for a 2025 launch, with no verified schedule."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"RAAN of 27.33° is described as indicating the launch facility location, which is incorrect; no epoch or LTAN (local time of ascending node) is given."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"Altitude reasoning is inconsistent: a very high altitude is called desirable for low resolution, and no swath width or GSD calculation supports the 600 km choice."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"States the only manoeuvre is at EOL, yet the EOL strategy is passive, so manoeuvre requirements are contradictory."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","issue":"Claims that proximity to the poles maximises pass frequency, then selects Tasmania (about 42°S) without reconciling this."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","issue":"Claims the descending pass is 'constantly in sunlight', which overstates illumination and ignores seasonal eclipse or dawn-dusk considerations."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","issue":"Access table lists access number 13 twice; otherwise the duration total and mean (8711.797 s, 580.786 s) are arithmetically correct."},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","issue":"Drag-sail paragraph is truncated mid-sentence, and the EOL recommendation is inconsistent with the earlier uncontrolled-descent reasoning."},{"where":"Bibliography","issue":"Uses Wikipedia (2021_in_spaceflight), which the brief explicitly forbids, and in-text citations [49]–[74] are missing from the list."}],"overall":"The report covers most required segments with reasonable STK evidence, but it has significant technical inconsistencies (launch plan, RAAN, manoeuvres, ground-station rationale), missing STK files, and serious citation failures including a forbidden Wikipedia source. Presentation errors and truncated text further reduce quality.","criteria":[{"id":"C1","level":"Accomplished","mark":7.0,"scale":10.0,"points":7.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"Primary mission, rationale, single-spacecraft choice with justification and likely customers are all addressed in detail with supporting literature. The operator is never clearly named, and the cited sources are mostly web pages rather than strong literature, so this sits at the lower end of Accomplished.","evidence":[{"where":"Introduction","quote":"Coral reefs, specifically the Great Barrier Reef, would immensely benefit from dedicated satellite monitoring."},{"where":"Introduction","quote":"Other Earth observation satellites use networks or constellations of satellites"},{"where":"Introduction","quote":"The proposed satellite would likely be used by governmental and science agencies (e.g. CSIRO, BOM) for research and/or education."}]},{"id":"C2","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":30.0,"max":60.0,"reasoning":"Most required elements are present, with STK access statistics that check out arithmetically and several screenshots. However the launch plan is inconsistent (piggyback versus direct launch, unbuilt Vega E, a 2021 manifest used for a 2025 launch). The RAAN explanation is wrong, the ground-station rationale contradicts Tasmania's latitude, and altitude choice lacks quantitative swath or GSD calculations. No STK files are provided.","evidence":[{"where":"Executive Summary","quote":"a direct launch into a retrograde sun-synchronous polar orbit"},{"where":"Launch","quote":"it should instead partake in the common practice of 'piggybacking'"},{"where":"Orbit","quote":"The initial value indicates the location of the launch facility."},{"where":"Orbit","quote":"the only orbital manoeuvre occurs at the EOL segment of the satellite's mission"},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","quote":"selecting a ground facility close to the poles"},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","quote":"Systems Tool Kit 11 (STK) was used to calculate access periods as seen below:"}]},{"id":"C3","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":5.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"The conclusion restates the design and flags the Vega E risk with a fallback, which is reasonable. It is largely a summary, however, and does not draw specific conclusions from the access statistics, EOL trade-off or sensitivity to the launch uncertainty. It also overstates the mission's effect on bleaching prevention.","evidence":[{"where":"Conclusion","quote":"A specific launch vehicle – the Vega E – has been selected although it has not been built yet."},{"where":"Conclusion","quote":"A polar sun-synchronous orbit allows for consistent illumination of the oceans while maintaining a constant sun angle."}]},{"id":"C4","level":"Adequate","mark":4.0,"scale":10.0,"points":8.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"Headings and a contents list give a clear structure, but there are many spelling and grammar errors and at least one truncated sentence in EOL. Tables and figures are not numbered or captioned properly. In-text citations [49]–[74] have no matching bibliography entries, a Wikipedia source is used, and reference entries are malformed. Presentation falls short of Accomplished.","evidence":[{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","quote":"It would therefore make sense to choose an uncontrolled descent as an end-of-life strategy"},{"where":"Bibliography","quote":"[5] f. https://www.qrg.northwestern.edu"},{"where":"Bibliography","quote":"[10 d."},{"where":"Launch","quote":"according to the in-text citation [50][51] in the launch table"},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","quote":"Mount Pleasant Radio Observatory)"}]}]}
